answer:
Because the language of the browser became the language of the tools that drive it. One install of Node gave you the same syntax for unit tests, API tests and end to end tests, so a team stopped maintaining three toolchains.

code:
const stack = {
  unitTests: 'vitest or node:test',
  apiTests: 'supertest against node',
  browserTests: 'playwright against node',
};
console.log(stack);
console.log('one language, one install, one runner family');

real example:
A team that moved from Java Selenium to Playwright kept the assertion style and lost the driver setup step, and the only new thing to learn was JavaScript itself.

Playwright is a Node library that drives Chromium, Firefox and WebKit through a single API, and it waits for conditions instead of sleeping. That auto-waiting is the main reason it produces stable suites.

code:
const promiseText = 'Playwright waits for the DOM, not for a timeout';
console.log(promiseText);
const api = { engines: ['chromium', 'firefox', 'webkit'], control: 'protocol level', waits: 'auto' };
console.log(api);
console.log('one API, three engines, no sleep calls needed');

real example:
A login test clicks submit and immediately asserts on the dashboard, and it passes on the first run because Playwright retried the assertion until the page settled.

Cypress is a browser based testing tool that runs inside the page context, so it can see and control the application the way a user does. It runs in a single browser engine rather than several.

code:
const cypress = { runsIn: 'the browser page context', engines: 'chromium based browsers', speed: 'fast on app code' };
console.log(cypress);
console.log('great visibility, limited engine coverage');
console.log('web workers and multi tab scenarios are awkward here');

real example:
A component test in Cypress can read a value straight out of a framework store, which Playwright would have to do from outside the page with an exposed hook.

Selenium is a cross language tool that controls a browser by sending commands over the WebDriver protocol, and it runs in a separate process from the page. That extra hop makes it slower but very widely supported.

code:
const selenium = {
  control: 'webdriver protocol',
  process: 'separate from the page',
  languages: ['java', 'python', 'c sharp', 'javascript'],
};
console.log(selenium);
console.log('broad reach, slower, more setup than a protocol level tool');

real example:
An older Selenium suite works today in every language but still needs driver binaries, a matching browser version and explicit waits, which is the cost of the protocol boundary.

Playwright drives the browser from outside through its debugging protocol and supports several engines. Cypress runs your test code inside the page next to the application code and uses one engine family.

code:
const compare = {
  playwright: { side: 'outside the page', engines: 3, tabs: 'native' },
  cypress: { side: 'inside the page', engines: 1, tabs: 'awkward' },
};
console.log(compare);
console.log('choose by what you must test, not by which one is newer');

real example:
A suite that opens a second tab and waits on a popup cannot be written in Cypress, so teams that need that behaviour end up on Playwright.

Selenium goes through a language neutral web standard, which makes it a good fit when your stack is not web based at all. Playwright talks to each engine directly, which is faster and less brittle but JavaScript only.

code:
const pick = (appLanguage) =>
  appLanguage === 'javascript' ? 'playwright' : 'selenium';
console.log(pick('javascript'));
console.log(pick('python'));
console.log('the deciding question is usually the language of your application');

real example:
A team testing a Java service already has Java skills and a Maven build, so staying with Selenium costs less than learning a second language for the same coverage.

WebdriverIO is a higher level runner built on top of webdriver style commands, and it bundles services, reporting and retries. Pick it when you want a full test platform rather than a library.

code:
const wdio = { base: 'webdriver protocol', givesYou: ['config', 'services', 'reporters', 'retries'] };
const playwright = { base: 'protocol level driver', givesYou: ['library', 'fixtures', 'auto waiting'] };
console.log(wdio.givesYou.join(', '));
console.log(playwright.givesYou.join(', '));
console.log('platform versus library, both are valid answers');

real example:
A large suite that needs a shared config file, screenshots on failure and a standard reporter gets all of that from WebdriverIO configuration instead of custom scripts.

Browser automation used to need a compiled language and a driver binary, so teams picked whatever their application used. When Node matured, one runtime installed everything and the learning cost dropped sharply.

code:
const before = { runtime: 'JVM or Python', setup: 'driver binaries', testFile: 'compiled or interpreted separately' };
const now = { runtime: 'node', setup: 'one install', testFile: 'plain text file next to the app' };
console.log(before.setup, '->', now.setup);
console.log('the barrier was the install, not the language itself');

real example:
A CI image that once cached a Maven repository and driver jars now caches one npm folder, which is why switching stacks often cuts pipeline minutes as well as learning time.

Anything that happens inside the page: event handlers, framework state, the DOM after a re-render, and timers. JavaScript tooling can read and drive the same objects the application code uses, without exposing hooks.

code:
const inside = [
  'read the dom after a re-render',
  'dispatch a custom event',
  'inspect a framework store',
  'measure timing with performance',
];
inside.forEach((item, i) => console.log(i + 1, item));
console.log('all of these are first class for in page tools');

real example:
A test for a cart badge can count matching elements in the DOM directly instead of clicking through the whole checkout flow, which keeps the test fast and specific.

No. It covers unit tests, API tests, database scripts, load tests and build steps too, because it is a general purpose language. The unit of work changes, the language does not.

code:
const layers = { unit: 'vitest', api: 'supertest', browser: 'playwright', scripts: 'node cli tools' };
Object.entries(layers).forEach(([layer, tool]) => console.log(layer.padEnd(8), tool));
console.log('one language, four kinds of work');

real example:
A Playwright fixture that creates test users through the same API helper the end to end test uses removes a duplicated setup file entirely.

Because the same language lets you share code between the app and the tests, and it removes the translation layer when reading failures. That shared code is where most of the saving comes from.

code:
const shared = ['validation helpers', 'data builders', 'api clients', 'selectors'];
shared.forEach((item) => console.log(item, '-> importable by both app and tests'));
console.log('same language means shared modules with no glue code');

real example:
A page object that imports the app own route table catches a renamed route as a failing test instead of as a bug reported by a customer.

It can, but the sharing advantage disappears and you gain a new language to learn. It still makes sense when the front end is JavaScript, since that is where most user interface bugs live.

code:
const appStack = { backend: 'java', frontend: 'javascript' };
const tests = { apiTests: 'match the backend for helpers', uiTests: 'match the frontend for selectors' };
console.log(appStack);
console.log(tests);
console.log('split the decision by layer instead of picking one language');

real example:
A Java backend tested with Supertest for smoke checks and Playwright for the user journey is a normal, workable split even when the backend team never writes JavaScript.

The runner loads your file as a module, calls your test registration functions, and collects the named tests into a list. It then runs each one, catches errors, and reports pass or fail with a duration.

code:
const registry = [];
function test(name, fn) { registry.push({ name, fn }); }
test('adds two rows', () => console.log('running body of the test'));
console.log('registered:', registry.length, 'test');
registry.forEach((t) => { console.log('->', t.name); t.fn(); });

real example:
Every Playwright test, it or test, is really a registration call, which is why defining a test inside a function that never runs means it silently never runs.

It gives you ordering, setup and teardown hooks, retries, reporting, parallelism and a clear pass or fail summary per test. A plain script gives you console output and an exit code.

code:
const run = (tests) => {
  const results = tests.map((t) => {
    try { t.fn(); return { name: t.name, ok: true }; }
    catch (e) { return { name: t.name, ok: false, error: e.message }; }
  });
  return results;
};
const results = run([
  { name: 'one', fn: () => { if (1 + 1 !== 2) throw new Error('bad math'); } },
  { name: 'two', fn: () => { throw new Error('element missing'); } },
]);
console.log(results.map((r) => r.name + ': ' + (r.ok ? 'pass' : 'fail')).join(', '));

real example:
A failed test in CI needs a named report and an exit code, not a stack trace printed on a build server nobody is watching.

The syntax is the easy part. The real work is objects and arrays, the event loop with promises and async, and the npm tooling, which has no equivalent in a compiled language build.

code:
const unfamiliar = [
  'objects and destructuring',
  'closures and callbacks',
  'promises and async functions',
  'npm, package.json, scripts',
];
unfamiliar.forEach((topic, i) => console.log(i + 1, topic));
console.log('expect weeks, not days, and mostly on the tooling side');

real example:
A Selenium Java tester can write locators on day one, then loses two weeks to module resolution and an npm install that fails on a proxy.

Because npm packages are numerous, they arrive with their own dependencies, and the popular tools change versions often. Teams get a large install and a constant upgrade chore.

code:
const trade = {
  install: 'a few hundred packages for a full stack',
  upgrades: 'several tools to keep in step',
  benefit: 'shared code with the application',
};
console.log(trade.install);
console.log('the benefit is real, so the cost is worth it for a web team');
console.log('pin versions in the lock file and the cost drops sharply');

real example:
A locked package file makes a CI install deterministic, so a suite that passed last month does not fail today because a helper package published a breaking release.

When the product is not a web page, when the team already has strong skills elsewhere, or when a company standard exists. Mobile, desktop, embedded and machine learning products still lean on other languages.

code:
const signals = [
  'the product is not a website',
  'the team already knows the stack well',
  'an internal tool already covers it',
  'no front end means no sharing benefit',
];
signals.forEach((s) => console.log('-', s));
console.log('any one of these is a good reason to stay');

real example:
A team automating a Windows desktop installer in C sharp should not rewrite its suite in JavaScript just because Playwright is popular on the internet.

You import the module directly, so the test and the app run the same function on the same input. The catch is that a broken helper now breaks both, which is information you want.

code:
const validators = {
  isEmail: (v) => typeof v === 'string' && v.includes('@') && v.includes('.'),
};
const tests = [
  { input: 'a@b.co', expected: true },
  { input: 'nope', expected: false },
];
tests.forEach((t) => {
  const actual = validators.isEmail(t.input);
  console.log(t.input, actual === t.expected ? 'pass' : 'fail');
});

real example:
Importing the real signup validation into a test means a rule change shows up as a test failure rather than as a support ticket from users.

A shared bug becomes a shared blind spot, because both sides agree on the wrong answer. Also, tests become coupled to internal structure, so a refactor breaks tests that were really testing the UI.

code:
function priceWithTax(p) { return p * 1.2; }
const testAssertion = priceWithTax(100) === 120;
console.log('test passes because it used the same function:', testAssertion);
console.log('but the real rule may be 120.00 with rounding');
console.log('keep shared helpers small, and test behaviour too');

real example:
Rounding money in the shared helper means both the app and the tests hide the same rounding error, which is why an independent expected value matters for critical logic.

Builders that mirror real domain rules belong near the application so they stay accurate. Fixtures that exist only to make a test pass belong in the test folder so nobody mistakes them for real logic.

code:
const domainHelper = { role: 'admin', active: true };
const testOnlyFixture = { role: 'admin', active: true, note: 'never used in prod' };
console.log('domain helper belongs to the app:', domainHelper);
console.log('test fixture belongs to the suite:', testOnlyFixture.note);

real example:
A user builder imported from the app repository lets a test create a valid admin, while a fixture living in the test folder can create an impossible admin that hides validation bugs.

Describe the gap honestly, then show what you have already built in JavaScript: scripts, helpers, small libraries and one real test suite. Concrete artifacts beat adjectives.

code:
const claim = {
  gap: 'no production framework experience yet',
  proof: ['a custom reporter for a test runner', 'a page object library used by three suites', 'a CI script that shards the suite'],
};
console.log(claim.gap);
claim.proof.forEach((p) => console.log('proof:', p));

real example:
Saying I write JavaScript daily for fixture tooling and selectors is verifiable in the repository, while saying I am proficient in Node is not.

Three areas: how the test runner reports and exits, how asynchronous waiting avoids flaky tests, and how the page under test actually behaves. Those three cover most real conversations.

code:
const depth = [
  'runner: reporting, retries, exit codes, sharding',
  'async: promises, auto waiting, avoiding race conditions',
  'browser: dom, events, storage, network',
];
depth.forEach((d, i) => console.log(i + 1, d));
console.log('each of these has a real story from a past project');

real example:
Explaining why a retry hid a genuine product bug and how you made the retry threshold visible is a far stronger answer than naming every Playwright method.

It is the combination of language, test runner, browser driver, reporting and CI that a project uses. Changing one part of the stack usually forces changes to the others.

code:
const stack = {
  language: 'javascript',
  runner: 'playwright test',
  driver: 'bundled chromium',
  reporting: 'html reporter',
  ci: 'github actions',
};
console.log(stack);
console.log('swap the runner and you rewrite config, reporters and hooks');

real example:
Moving a suite from Cypress to Playwright is not a library swap, it is a stack change, which is why it takes weeks rather than an afternoon.

Because the language has the most users and the most frameworks, so more problems get asked and more get solved. That is a genuine advantage when you are blocked on an error.

code:
const reasons = ['most used web language', 'most frameworks', 'most blogs and videos', 'built in error messages'];
reasons.forEach((r, i) => console.log(i + 1, r));
console.log('search first, then ask, it often saves the whole afternoon');

real example:
A stack overflow type question about Playwright locators usually has an answer from a maintainer, which is rarer for Selenium bindings in other languages.

Because a single Node binary pulls the toolchain into any machine, container or pipeline image with one command. There is no separate runtime to install and keep in step with the tests.

code:
const pipeline = ['install node', 'run install command', 'run test command', 'read the exit code'];
pipeline.forEach((step, i) => console.log(i + 1, step));
console.log('no jvm, no driver setup, nothing version paired to remember');

real example:
A pipeline step that runs tests on three operating systems only changes the runner command, because the runner itself is a portable binary plus a lock file.

You get assertions, loops, data setup and reuse of your own helper modules, plus the tests run in CI with the browser tests. Postman is a good explorer, but it is not a test suite.

code:
const request = (path) => ({ url: '/api' + path, method: 'GET', auth: 'bearer token' });
const cases = ['/users/1', '/users/999', '/orders/1'];
cases.forEach((c) => {
  const req = request(c);
  console.log(req.method, req.url, 'assert status and body shape');
});

real example:
A Supertest check that every user response has an id field catches a backend rename that a Postman collection with two manual checks would miss.

Unit tests call your functions directly and run in milliseconds. API tests start a server or hit a running one, and end to end tests drive a real browser. Each is slower and closer to the truth.

code:
const layers = [
  { kind: 'unit', runs: 'vitest', speed: 'fast', covers: 'logic' },
  { kind: 'api', runs: 'supertest', speed: 'medium', covers: 'contracts' },
  { kind: 'end to end', runs: 'playwright', speed: 'slow', covers: 'user journeys' },
];
layers.forEach((l) => console.log(l.kind.padEnd(10), l.runs.padEnd(9), l.speed));
console.log('put most of the checks in the fast layer');

real example:
Moving a discount rule from an end to end test to a unit test turns a thirty second browser test into a thirty millisecond one, which is why the fast layer grows first.

The language is single threaded inside one process, but a runner can start several worker processes or use separate browser contexts. Each worker gets its own copy of the JavaScript engine.

code:
const parallel = { oneProcess: ['test a', 'test b'], manyWorkers: [['test a'], ['test b']] };
console.log('single process runs them one after another');
console.log('four workers can run four browser tests together');
console.log('speed comes from more processes, not more threads per process');

real example:
Sharding a thousand browser tests across four CI machines cuts the job to roughly a quarter of the time, as long as each shard creates its own test data.

Anything shared: a fixed user name, a single test account, a file on disk or a database row. The tests then overwrite each other and fail in a different order each run.

code:
function userName(worker, index) { return 'user-' + worker + '-' + index; }
const workers = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const clashes = workers.flatMap((w) => [userName(w, 1), userName(w, 1)]);
console.log('unique per worker:', workers.map((w) => userName(w, 1)));
console.log('shared name, used by both:', clashes[0], clashes[1]);

real example:
Two shards logging in as the same demo user see each other sessions and the second assertion fails, so a good fixture appends the worker id to every identity.

You may end up with a second language nobody on the team can debug at three in the morning, and a toolchain that changes under you. Trend is not a reason; the app stack and the team are.

code:
const decision = ['does the app run on javascript', 'can the team debug it', 'will the tools still exist in a year'];
decision.forEach((q, i) => console.log(i + 1, q));
console.log('all three yes, choose it; otherwise think twice');

real example:
A team that chose Playwright for a Python only product now has four engineers who cannot debug a failing browser test without asking the one person who set it up.

Three inputs: the language of the application, the skills already on the team, and how much time the suite has to run. Everything else is preference and can be argued later.

code:
const inputs = {
  appLanguage: 'javascript',
  teamSkills: ['java', 'javascript'],
  timeBudget: 'under ten minutes',
};
const chosen = inputs.appLanguage === 'javascript' ? 'playwright plus vitest' : 'selenium in the app language';
console.log('inputs:', inputs);
console.log('chosen stack:', chosen);

real example:
The ten minute budget rules out fifty browser tests per pull request and pushes most checks down to the fast layer, which is a tooling decision driven by CI cost.
